---
import Brand from '../components/Brand.astro';
import HeroHeadline from '../components/HeroHeadline.astro';
import ContractConsole from '../components/ContractConsole.astro';
import TechnicalFigure from '../components/TechnicalFigure.astro';
import LanguageTour from '../components/LanguageTour.astro';
import LiveEvolution from '../components/LiveEvolution.astro';
import SiteNavigation from '../components/SiteNavigation.astro';
import { project } from '../lib/project';
import PremiumPagePreview from '../components/PremiumPagePreview.astro';
import FreePagePreview from '../components/FreePagePreview.astro';
import '../styles/custom.css';
import '../styles/landing.css';
const tutorial = '/learn/01-first-program/';
const features = [
  { n: '01', title: 'Ownership and effects', text: 'A signature lists the effects a function can perform and the data it borrows. A higher-order function takes on the effects of its callback, so mapping a logging function over a list is a logging computation, and the type says so.', href: '/learn/12-ownership-and-regions/', link: 'Tutorial 12: Ownership, borrowing, and regions' },
  { n: '02', title: 'Structured concurrency', text: 'Every task belongs to a scope and finishes before the scope does. Channels are bounded and move ownership of what they carry, so a full channel pushes back on its sender. Cancellation, joining, and cleanup each have their own contract.', href: '/learn/17-structured-concurrency/', link: 'Tutorial 17: Structured tasks and cancellation' },
  { n: '03', title: 'Temporal contracts', text: 'State what a service must always preserve and what it must eventually do, against an abstract state machine. Assumptions about the scheduler and the environment are written into the contract.', href: '/learn/21-upgrades-and-contracts/', link: 'Tutorial 21: State evolution and temporal contracts' },
  { n: '04', title: 'Productive streams', text: 'Infinite data is codata. The checker proves that a stream always produces its next element. A search that might never find a match gets a weaker type, because it might never return.', href: '/learn/15-streams-and-producers/', link: 'Tutorial 15: Productive streams and partial producers' },
  { n: '05', title: 'Foreign code and Wasm', text: 'A foreign call declares its ABI, who owns each pointer, which effects it performs, and how it fails. WebAssembly component interfaces carry the same information for resource handles.', href: '/learn/20-foreign-code-and-targets/', link: 'Tutorial 20: Foreign code, layouts, and target profiles' },
];
---
<!doctype html>
<html lang="en" class="cinch-landing" data-theme="dark">
  <head>
    <meta charset="UTF-8" /><meta name="viewport" content="width=device-width" />
    <title>Cinch — Power, with proof.</title>
    <link rel="canonical" href={new URL('/', Astro.site)} />
    <meta name="description" content="Cinch is a strict functional language whose types say what a function needs, what it does, and what it borrows. The compiler checks those claims before the program runs." />
    <meta name="theme-color" content="#191a17" />
    <link rel="icon" href="/favicon.svg" type="image/svg+xml" />
    <link rel="preload" href="/fonts/momo-trust-display.ttf" as="font" type="font/ttf" crossorigin />
    <link rel="preload" href="/fonts/dm-sans-400.ttf" as="font" type="font/ttf" crossorigin />
  </head>
  <body>
    <a class="landing-skip" href="#main">Skip to content</a>
    <header class="landing-header">
      <div class="header-identity"><Brand /></div>
      <SiteNavigation />
    </header>
    <main id="main">
      <section class="hero" aria-labelledby="hero-title">
                <div class="hero-grid">
          <div class="hero-copy">
            <div class="hero-stripes" aria-hidden="true"><span></span><span></span><span></span></div>
            <HeroHeadline />
            <p class="hero-description">A strict functional language whose types say what a function needs, what it does, and what it borrows.</p>
            <p class="hero-detail">The compiler checks every one of those claims before the program runs. A Cinch program that compiles does not crash: anything that can still go wrong appears in its type, and you handle it.</p>
            <div class="hero-actions"><a class="button button-primary" href="/downloads/">Get Cinch <span>↗</span></a><a class="text-link" href={tutorial}>Write your first program <span>→</span></a></div>
          </div>
          <ContractConsole />
        </div>
        <div class="hero-baseline mono"><a href="#why-cinch">INSIDE THE LANGUAGE <span>↓</span></a></div>
      </section>
      <section class="manifesto light-section" id="why-cinch" aria-labelledby="manifesto-title">
        <div class="section-index mono"><span>THE LANGUAGE</span></div>
        <div class="manifesto-grid"><h2 id="manifesto-title">Familiar code.<br /><span>Stronger types.</span></h2><div class="manifesto-copy"><p>If you have written Haskell or OCaml, most Cinch code will look familiar. What changes is how much a type can say, and that the compiler holds the code to it.</p><p>Algebraic data types, pattern matching, higher-order functions, and instances work as you would expect. Evaluation is strict. Ownership and borrowing manage memory and other resources without a tracing garbage collector. When you need a stronger guarantee, such as an index that is in bounds or a list that is never empty, you write it in the type and the compiler checks the proof. Routine arithmetic facts are proved automatically.</p><a class="text-link" href="/specifications/core-language-specification/">Core language specification <span>↗</span></a></div></div>
        <nav class="language-jump" aria-label="Explore language features"><a href="#proofs">Proofs and refinements ↓</a><a href="#specialization">Type specialization ↓</a><a href="#staging">Typed staging ↓</a><a href="#optimization">Verified optimization ↓</a><a href="#live-development">Incremental builds and hot reload ↓</a><a href="#runtime-contracts">Runtime contracts ↓</a></nav>
      </section>
      <div class="panel-joint" aria-hidden="true"><span></span><i></i><span></span></div>
      <section class="proof-section" aria-labelledby="proof-title">
        <div class="section-index mono"><span>DEPENDENT TYPES</span></div>
        <div class="proof-grid">
          <div class="proof-copy"><h2 id="proof-title">A first element <br />requires <br /><em>a nonempty vector.</em></h2><p>The input has length n + 1. An empty vector has length 0, so it cannot be passed to this function.</p><p class="muted">Pattern matching leaves one constructor to handle. The returned reference borrows from the input and cannot outlive it.</p><a class="text-link" href="/learn/14-dependent-types-and-proofs/#vectors-retain-a-length">Read the example in context <span>↗</span></a></div>
          <div class="code-window"><div class="code-toolbar mono"><span><i></i> first.cinch</span></div><pre aria-label="Cinch dependent type example"><code><span class="code-comment">-- Borrow the first element of a nonempty vector.</span>
<span class="code-name">first</span> <span class="code-punct">::</span> <span class="code-keyword">forall</span> a n region.
  <span class="code-type">Ref</span> region (<span class="code-type">Vec</span> (n + <span class="code-number">1</span>) a)
  <span class="code-punct">-&gt;</span> <span class="code-type">Ref</span> region a

<span class="code-name">first</span> xs = <span class="code-keyword">case</span> xs <span class="code-keyword">of</span>
  <span class="code-type">Cons</span> x rest <span class="code-punct">-&gt;</span> x</code></pre><div class="code-note"><span class="proof-check">↳</span><p><strong>Why there is no Nil branch</strong><br /><code>Nil</code> has length 0. This input requires <code>n + 1</code>.</p></div></div>
        </div>
        <TechnicalFigure id="landing-vector-figure" />
      </section>
      <LanguageTour />
      <LiveEvolution />
      <section class="feature-section light-section" id="runtime-contracts" aria-labelledby="features-title">
        <div class="section-index mono"><span>RUNTIME CONTRACTS</span></div>
        <h2 id="features-title">Carry the contract<br />into a running system.</h2>
        <div class="feature-list">{features.map(feature => <article class="feature-row"><span class="feature-number mono" aria-hidden="true">{feature.n}</span><div><h3>{feature.title}</h3></div><div><p>{feature.text}</p><a class="text-link" href={feature.href}>{feature.link}<span>↗</span></a></div></article>)}</div>
      </section>
      <section class="reading-section" aria-labelledby="reading-title">
        <div class="section-index mono"><span>DOCUMENTATION</span></div>
        <div class="reading-heading"><h2 id="reading-title">Learn the<br />language.</h2><p>Start with a working program.<br />Keep the compiler guide nearby.</p></div>
        <div class="reading-links"><a href={tutorial}><h3>Tutorials</h3><p>22 chapters, from a first executable to dependent proofs, each with worked programs and exercises.</p><span class="reading-arrow" aria-hidden="true">↗</span></a><a href="/reference/00-guide/"><h3>Compiler guide</h3><p>Syntax, checking rules, commands, and runtime controls.</p><span class="reading-arrow" aria-hidden="true">↗</span></a><a href="/library/00-index/"><h3>Library reference</h3><p>Every module that ships with the compiler, documented from its checked interface.</p><span class="reading-arrow" aria-hidden="true">↗</span></a></div>
        
      </section>
      <section class="closing"><div class="mono">TUTORIAL 1 / BUILD AND RUN A PROGRAM</div><a href={tutorial}><h2>Write your<br /><span>first program.</span></h2><span class="closing-arrow" aria-hidden="true">↗</span></a></section>
    </main>
    <footer class="landing-footer"><Brand /><div><a href="/documentation/">Documentation</a><a href="/packages/">Packages</a><a href="/downloads/">Downloads</a><a href={project.repository}>GitHub ↗</a><a href="#main">Back to top ↑</a></div></footer>
    <PremiumPagePreview />
    <FreePagePreview />
  </body>
</html>
